<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Osceola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a>–<a href="/years/1838">1838</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Osceola was a Seminole war leader who led resistance to removal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-seminole-war">Second Seminole War</a>. In 1837 he was seized by American troops while meeting them under a flag of truce, an act widely condemned. He died in prison at Fort Moultrie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> in 1838 and became a lasting symbol of Native resistance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/osceola.jpg" alt="Osceola" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Catlin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Catlin_-_Os-ce-o-l%C3%A1,_The_Black_Drink,_a_Warrior_of_Great_Distinction_-_1985.66.301_-_Smithsonian_American_Art_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Osceola was born Billy Powell in 1804 in the Upper Creek village of Talisi, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tallapoosa-river">Tallapoosa River</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>. His mother, Polly Coppinger, was a mixed-race Muscogee Creek woman, and his father was most likely William Powell, a Scottish trader. Because the Muscogee reckoned kinship through the mother, he was raised in his mother's clan and in Muscogee tradition.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> forces defeated the Red Stick Muscogee in 1814, Polly took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, where they joined the Seminole. His adult name, Osceola, is an anglicised form of the Creek Vsse Yvholv, combining the ceremonial black drink and a word for the one who performs a special whoop at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-corn-ceremony">Green Corn Ceremony</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Settlers pressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to remove the Seminole from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. In 1832 a few chiefs signed the Treaty of Payne's Landing, giving up their land for territory west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a>. By legend Osceola stabbed the treaty with his knife, though no contemporary report says so. Five leading chiefs, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micanopy">Micanopy</a>, refused removal, and the Indian agent Wiley Thompson declared them deposed and banned the sale of guns and ammunition to the Seminole. Osceola resented the ban as equating the Seminole with slaves. After Thompson had him locked up at Fort King for two nights, he agreed to sign the treaty to secure release.</p><p>On 28 December 1835 Osceola killed Thompson with the rifle Thompson had given him and shot six others outside Fort King, while another group of Seminole ambushed and killed more than 100 soldiers on the march from Fort Brooke, an event Americans called the Dade Massacre. These attacks began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-seminole-war">Second Seminole War</a>. In April 1836 Osceola led warriors in a siege of Fort Cooper, which held out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 21 October 1837 General Joseph Hernández, on the orders of General Thomas Jesup, seized Osceola and 81 followers as they came under a white flag of truce for talks near Fort Peyton. He was held at Fort Marion in St. Augustine and then at Fort Moultrie near Charleston, where the artists George Catlin and Robert John Curtis painted him. Many congressional leaders condemned the capture, and Jesup's reputation suffered. Osceola, ill with malaria and tonsillitis, died of quinsy on 30 January 1838. His doctor, Frederick Weedon, cut off his head instead of returning the body to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> as Osceola had asked. Counties in Florida, Iowa and Michigan, Osceola National Forest and several towns bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Osceola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Second Seminole War of 1835 to 1842, led by <b>Osceola</b> and Micanopy, the Seminole fought the United States to a costly standstill, but most were removed to Indian Territory, where Wewoka became their capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/seminole-nation">Seminole Nation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Georgia, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Fort Moultrie, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33,-83.5&amp;year=1826" aria-label="Georgia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2411 965 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Osceola"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2861 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2861 1235h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33,-83.5&amp;year=1826">What was here in 1826? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
